... anyway C only for now. We have a thread pool architecture, and a concept of
"boxes" which are a datastructure that stores a function pointer, some memory
locations, ring buffers, etc. There is also a concept of a "map" which is a
collection of these boxes connected together. That's the bulk of the design,
and all the extra stuff we added for visualization and cross-language bridges is
not part of the core design.

there is also a concept of different box types, but we really only need a few
of those. Mostly a comparator (put the result in this location if </>/= a value,
often derived from within the box itself or given as input) which routes the
result to a specified location. We also have an iterator, which sends the output
first to one target, then on subsequent runs to another, then on the next run to
a third, etc, until we get to the bottom and then it loops to the top for the
next run. I think those are the only box types we need.

Oh, we also need to be able to "read" and "write" to files. These should be
special box types, and the "read" should "hang" until a value is present, and
the "write" should hang until the target destination exists. I think this
behavior exists and can be written as a custom, non built in box, but it's worth
thinking about specifically.

boxes should only run in one single situation - when they're picked up from the
thread pool. Boxes should only be instantiated into the thread pool in one case:
if an input value was filled, and all of the input values were checked and
contained something. Then, the input-filler thread puts the task into the thread
pool and it gets picked up by someone else.

